"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[52977],{352977:e=>{e.exports="# Dashboards: Databricks AI/BI Dashboards\n\nDomain knowledge for creating and managing Databricks AI/BI Dashboards.\n\n## Visualization Types\n\n| Chart Type     | Best For                           |\n| -------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| **Bar Chart**  | Comparing values across categories |\n| **Line Chart** | Showing trends over time           |\n| **Counter**    | Single key metrics / KPIs          |\n| **Table**      | Detailed data in rows and columns  |\n| **Pie Chart**  | Proportions of a whole             |\n\n### Selection Guide\n\n- Comparing discrete categories → Bar chart\n- Time-series data → Line chart\n- KPIs and summary metrics → Counter\n- Detail matters more than visual patterns → Table\n\n## Dashboard Design Principles\n\n### Information Hierarchy\n\n- Place most important metrics at top or center\n- Use size and position to indicate importance\n- Group related information together\n- Create clear visual flow: top-to-bottom, left-to-right\n\n### Layout\n\n- Use consistent spacing between widgets\n- Align widgets to a grid for clean appearance\n- Limit widgets per page to ~100 for performance\n- Balance dense and sparse areas\n\n### Dashboard Types by Purpose\n\n- **Executive Summary**: Counters and simple charts\n- **Operational**: Real-time data and alerts\n- **Analytical**: Detailed tables and complex visualizations\n- **Report**: Tables and detailed breakdowns\n\n## Dataset Design\n\n- Ensure a suitable dataset (SQL query) exists before creating widgets\n- Verify dataset schema matches visualization needs\n- Optimize queries for speed — use appropriate aggregation levels\n- Bind widget data fields to dataset columns with correct aggregations\n\n## How to Work with Dashboards via the Databricks Agent\n\nThe Dashboard Authoring Agent on the dashboard page has specialized widget-editing tools. For dashboard work from the Databricks Agent:\n\n### Creating a New Dashboard\n\n1. Use `createAsset({ assetType: 'dashboard', name: 'My Dashboard' })` to create it\n2. Use `openAsset({ assetType: 'dashboard', assetId: '<id>' })` immediately with a `continueMessage` relaying the user's goal and any known context\n3. Do NOT search for tables, read schemas, or plan widgets before handing off — the Dashboard Authoring Agent handles all data discovery\n\n### Editing an Existing Dashboard\n\n1. Use `readAssetById` to inspect current dashboard content\n2. Use `openAsset` to navigate to the dashboard with a `continueMessage` for the Dashboard Authoring Agent\n3. The `continueMessage` should describe in detail the user's goals as understood so far\n\n### Best Practices for Handoff\n\n- Include specific known or discussed details in `continueMessage`: table names, column names, chart types, filters\n- For multi-step workflows (e.g., analyze data then create dashboard): complete analysis first, then hand off\n"}}]);
